<p>I&rsquo;ve been doing a bit of sewing recently after a bit of a break in which I really <a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/no-bounce-in-my-bungee/">didn&rsquo;t have the energy</a>. I still have to take it much slower than I used to do &mdash; no more non-stop sewing days &mdash; but I am finding that if I spread cutting out and sewing over several days, I can manage it.</p>
<p>This is actually quite nice as it forces me to take my time and pay attention to the details. In the process, I have noticed some seemingly rigid &lsquo;Laws of Sewing&rsquo; over a few projects.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>If you make a mistake, it will be on the overlap side rather than the underlap</strong>. Buttonholes are obviously the most frequent offenders here<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup> because they are almost always on the overlapping side, but the seam junction of the waistband on jeans and other trousers/pants are other places where this turns up. Even more maddeningly, this often also involves Law No. 2.</li>
<li><strong>The element on the &lsquo;practice&rsquo; side will be perfect and beautiful, but the next one will be a hot mess</strong>. You&rsquo;ll decide (sensibly) to start something tricky where it will be less visible &mdash; say, finishing the edge of the waistband of a pair of jeans which will be the underlapping side. That part will be stunning, but the overlapping side you sew while full of confidence after the &lsquo;practice&rsquo; will be wonky as all heck. This also happens when you practice on a scrap of fabric first. I wasn&rsquo;t sure if my automatic buttonhole foot would be able to cope with topstitch thread on denim fabric, so I tested on a scrap with multiple layers of denim, so that it was suitably realistic. That was the most gorgeous buttonhole I have ever seen in my life. When I sewed it on the actual jeans, the first side of the buttonhole was similarly gorgeous, but the second side made it look like my machine had visited the pub for a few pints before heading back for the return leg. I did what pragmatic sewists always do in these circumstances: smother it with Fray Check and console yourself with the fact that the button will probably cover the worst of it.</li>
<li><strong>On every project, there will be one element which always turns out badly but goes incredibly well, and another that you have executed beautifully many times which goes horribly wrong this time</strong>. This continues to amaze me. For example, I recently made a shirt from a pattern that I have made before. It has tower plackets on the sleeves which I always struggle to make neatly. I also often find it difficult to make the collar points and cuffs symmetrical and nicely pointy. This time, that all went like a dream, to the point where I find myself examining the tower plackets on this shirt and smiling dreamily to myself because they are so lovely and neat. However, while I have sewn many a collar stand on to a neckline (indeed, using this very same pattern) without any drama at all, this time the neckline was somehow way longer than the stand, even though I staystitched the neckline like a responsible citizen. I have no idea what went wrong, but I had to seam rip that collar stand out a couple of times before I got it attached.</li>
</ol>
<p>Despite all this, I continue to find sewing a joy, even if it sometimes involves a lot of swearing.</p>
